 my business  registration or any formal training  you just need logical strategies of  survival it wasn't just about where I  was what I saw looking out the window on  a seven-hour journey in the Philippines  wasn't different from what I saw on a  two-hour train ride in India and a  seven-hour bash trip in Mexico and it  wasn't different from what I was seeing  in Ghana farmers intro revealed to me  the fabric of the local economy which I  completely ignored in giving him advice  our conversation was about the informal  economy the term informal economy refers  to any economic activity that is in law  or in practice not sufficiently covered  by formal arrangements there are 2  billion people employed in the informal  economy which is 60% of the world's  working population it is mostly present  in developing countries but it exists  everywhere else in the world in advanced  economies like the US and Europe  humanity is grappling with a fear of AI  and robotics technologies and the  question on everyone's mind is how can  we adapt to this challenge mobility  revolution my answer is learn about the  informal economy it has a place in the  world where people are experts in  embracing and artists in navigating  life's difficulties I believe learning  about a informal economy it's helpful in  preparing us towards a future  where most of us would be self-employed  yes you're currently 162 million people  was self-employed in US and in Europe  for some of them it is out of choice but  for others it is out of necessity  whichever your position is here at three  skills you can learn from people who are  working in the informal economy to help  you better prepare for the future of  work  the first skill is adaptability the  Canadian market is the largest open-air  market in West Africa and it is located  in my hometown of Massey  it has 45,000 stores made out of  concrete floors wooden slabs and  aluminum roofing sheets with a wide  variety of business activities it faces  at least three major fire outbreaks  every year after every fire store owned  as well count their losses but go back  to what the nez day like nothing  happened on one such occasion I visited  a market two days after fire gutted 150  stores I was standing right in the spot  but to my surprise there was no sign of  the fire rather fresh paint and business  as usual there are so many shocks in the  informal economy but fear and  uncertainty isn't one of them to move  forward in times of disruption we would  need to learn how to embrace chaos in  order to adapt the second skill I would  like to share is diversification one  thing that surprises me about living in  the u.s. is most people tend to focus  pretty much on one career I come from a  culture where almost everyone has a side  hustle or side gig the concept of a side  hustle is having a blend of jobs it's  like having a full-time job and a  part-time job or having multiple  independent jobs just like the street  switches from seven ice water to  umbrellas just by studying the weather  having a diversified career its  hopefully not just in shifts in the  market one thing that is very addictive  which we talked a little about is the  concept of a salary if we have an  opportunity to generate multiple streams  of income take it it changes how you  think the last kill I would like to  discuss as relationships the informal  economy is a large network of people not  connected by business cars but by trust  my mother introduces herself as a  businesswoman period but let me provide  you more details she has a 95 job as a  high school administrator and a  portfolio of side hassles she as a kid I  remember she used to work she used to  buy clothes bill of clothes and  distributing small lots to fashion shops  she later went on into coconut oil  processing and she had to develop  relationships with food chains and  restaurants to sell through them seven  years ago she came up with an excellent  idea  she wanted to travel to 